On a curve, the polar curvature separates the singularity data of a model from the area of the background. A smooth gauge change reduces any background of positive area to a positive form. Keeping the polar measure fixed preserves distances whenever both backgrounds admit it, while rescaling by the ratio of the areas identifies the entire model spaces; the gauge function controls the normalized representatives. At zero area the whole model space collapses to the unique normalized potential. We keep the curve $Y$ of section 13.28 , and we fix a smooth positive $(1,1)$-form $\eta $ on $Y$ with $\int _ Y\eta =1$, which we use for $L^1$ norms and capacities. In this section any smooth real $(1,1)$-form $\sigma $ on $Y$ with $A_\sigma =\int _ Y\sigma \geq 0$ is allowed as a background. By lemma 5.29.22 , these are exactly the forms for which $\mathrm{PSH}(Y,\sigma )$ is nonempty, and $\sigma =A_\sigma \eta +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}h$ for a smooth real function $h$. Let $\mathcal M(\sigma )$ be the set of ordinary models in $\mathrm{PSH}(Y,\sigma )$, and put $U_\sigma (p)=\int _ Y\mathrm{MA}_\sigma (p)$. The volume comparison $d_\sigma $ is defined by the formula of definition 13.22.1 ; at zero area it vanishes identically. Polar curvatures, their atomic parts and ideal models are defined as before, with $\sigma $ in place of $	heta $. Proposition 13.29.1 . C9C98321BEFC Polar curvature identifies $\mathcal M(\sigma )$ with the set of polar measures of mass at most $A_\sigma $. Write $p_\sigma (\mu )$ for the model with polar measure $\mu $. Then \[  U_\sigma (p_\sigma (\mu ))=A_\sigma -\mu (Y),\qquad d_\sigma (p_\sigma (\mu ),p_\sigma (
u ))=\| \mu -
u \| _{\mathrm{TV}},  \] and ideal projection corresponds to taking the atomic part. The space $(\mathcal M(\sigma ),d_\sigma )$ is complete and geodesic, and convergence for $d_\sigma $ implies convergence in $L^1$ and in capacity. If $A_\sigma =0$, its only member is the smooth potential $u_\sigma $ of lemma 5.29.22 . Proof. Suppose first that $A=A_\sigma >0$, and write $\sigma =A\eta +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}h$. For $v\in \mathrm{PSH}(Y,A\eta )$, the inequalities for ordinary envelopes in proposition 18.5.1 give \[  P_{A\eta }[v]+\min _ Yh \leq P_\sigma [v-h]+h \leq P_{A\eta }[v]+\max _ Yh.  \] For a model $p$ in background $A\eta $, the potential $q=P_\sigma [p-h]$ therefore satisfies $q+h\sim p$, and since the envelope depends only on the ordinary type, $P_\sigma [q]=P_\sigma [p-h]=q$. Conversely, for $q\in \mathcal M(\sigma )$, the same bounds with $v=q+h$ show that $P_{A\eta }[q+h]\sim q+h$, and taking the envelope back recovers $q$. Thus $p\mapsto P_\sigma [p-h]$ is a bijection from $\mathcal M(A\eta )$ onto $\mathcal M(\sigma )$. Smooth gauge changes preserve curvature currents, and ordinary equivalence preserves their polar parts by corollary 5.27.15 . Consequently the bijection preserves polar curvature. It also preserves singularity volumes, and subtracting the same smooth function commutes with maxima. The classification and the distance formula of lemma 13.28.1 , theorem 13.28.4 , applied to the positive form $A\eta $, now give the first assertions. The same gauge bounds for ideal envelopes show that the transported ideal projections are ordinarily equivalent. Both are ordinary models by corollary 18.4.4 , and ordinary models of the same type are equal. Thus the bijection commutes with ideal projection, which still takes the atomic part. Completeness and the geodesics follow from the identification with polar measures, as in corollary 13.28.7 . For convergence to a model of positive mass, note that $\sigma $ is big because $\sigma +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}(-h)=A\eta >0$, and that the convergence of the polar masses gives a common positive lower-volume bound; then apply proposition 13.22.4 , theorem 13.23.3 . For a limit of zero mass, the current estimate ( 13.28.9 ) follows from the same polar decomposition as before. Normalized compactness and the uniqueness of harmonic functions give $L^1$ convergence, and lemma 5.27.11 on a finite cover gives convergence in capacity. If $A_\sigma =0$, lemma 5.29.22 leaves only $u_\sigma $ after normalization. Its curvature current vanishes, and both envelope operations fix it. All assertions reduce to statements about the zero measure and the one-point metric space. $\square$ The criterion for equality of the ordinary and ideal envelopes also depends only on the polar data, including at zero area. Corollary 13.29.2 . 02F73F083FCE For $u\in \mathrm{PSH}(Y,\sigma )$ put \[  q=P_\sigma [u]_{\mathcal I},\qquad \mu _ u=\mathbf1_{\{ u=-\infty \} }(\sigma +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}u),\qquad D_ u=\sum _{y\in Y}
u (u,y)\delta _ y.  \] The following are equivalent: \[   egin{gathered} u\in \mathcal E_{\mathrm{env}}(Y,\sigma ,q),\qquad \mu _ u=D_ u,\ P_\sigma [u]=P_\sigma [u]_{\mathcal I},\ P_{\sigma ,F}[u]=P_{\sigma ,F}[u]_{\mathcal I} \quad 	ext{for every smooth real }F. \end{gathered}  \] If $A_\sigma =0$, all four conditions hold for every $u\in \mathrm{PSH}(Y,\sigma )$. Proof. Suppose first that $A=A_\sigma >0$, and write $\sigma =A\eta +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}h$. Put $v=u+h$ and $q_+=P_{A\eta }[v]_{\mathcal I}$. The gauge change preserves the curvature current and its pole set, hence both $\mu _ u$ and $D_ u$. By proposition 18.5.1 , $q+h\sim q_+$. The gauge invariance of the relative envelope class and its invariance under equivalent references (proposition 16.28.62 ) give \[  u\in \mathcal E_{\mathrm{env}}(Y,\sigma ,q) \quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad v\in \mathcal E_{\mathrm{env}}(Y,A\eta ,q_+).  \] By corollary 13.28.23 , this is equivalent to $\mu _ u=D_ u$. The gauge bounds for the ordinary and ideal envelopes, corollary 5.27.15 and the classifications in the positive background $A\eta $ identify the polar curvatures of $P_\sigma [u]$ and $P_\sigma [u]_{\mathcal I}$ with $\mu _ u$ and $D_ u$, respectively. Both envelopes are ordinary models, the ideal one by corollary 18.4.4 . The classification of models in proposition 13.29.1 therefore shows that they are equal if and only if $\mu _ u=D_ u$. For a smooth real $F$, the bijection $a\mapsto a+h$ between competitors gives \[  P_{\sigma ,F}[u]+h=P_{A\eta ,F+h}[v],  \] and the same identity for the ideal envelopes. Indeed, this bijection preserves the obstacle bound, the ordinary order and multiplier-ideal equivalence, and addition of $h$ commutes with upper regularization. Since $F\mapsto F+h$ is a bijection of the smooth obstacles, corollary 13.28.23 gives the remaining equivalence. If $A_\sigma =0$, lemma 5.29.22 gives $u=-h+c$ for a constant $c$, and the curvature current of $u$ vanishes. Thus $\mu _ u=D_ u=0$. All potentials in this background are smooth and ordinarily equivalent, so their ordinary and ideal competitor families below every obstacle coincide, and they are nonempty after a constant translation. The reference $q$ is ordinarily equivalent to $u$, and proposition 16.28.62 gives $u\in \mathcal E_{\mathrm{env}}(Y,\sigma ,q)$. $\square$ Theorem 13.29.3 . 151BAA2FA1AB Let $	heta _0,	heta _1$ be smooth real $(1,1)$-forms on $Y$ with positive areas $A_ i=A_{	heta _ i}$, and put $\lambda =A_1/A_0$. The map \[  F_{10}:\mathcal M(	heta _0)\longrightarrow \mathcal M(	heta _1), \qquad F_{10}(p_{	heta _0}(\mu ))=p_{	heta _1}(\lambda \mu ),  \] is a bijection and satisfies 7ADEC26E190A 13.29.4  egin{equation}   egin{split}  d_{	heta _1}(F_{10}(p),F_{10}(q))& =\lambda d_{	heta _0}(p,q),\  U_{	heta _1}(F_{10}(p))& =\lambda U_{	heta _0}(p),\  P_{	heta _1}[F_{10}(p)]_{\mathcal I} & =F_{10}(P_{	heta _0}[p]_{\mathcal I}). \end{split} \end{equation} For a third smooth background $	heta _2$ of positive area, the same construction gives $F_{21}F_{10}=F_{20}$. Proof. Multiplication by $\lambda $ is a bijection from the polar measures of mass at most $A_0$ onto those of mass at most $A_1$. By proposition 13.29.1 , it gives the asserted bijection, and the distances satisfy \[  \| \lambda \mu -\lambda 
u \| _{\mathrm{TV}} =\lambda \| \mu -
