e y$, attain it. Consequently the unscaled spaces can be isometric only when their areas agree. Alternatively, $p_{	heta _0}(\mu )\mapsto p_{	heta _1}(\mu )$ is an isometry on the polar measures with $\mu (Y)\leq \min (A_0,A_1)$. The two models have the same ordinary type. Indeed, the Green representatives have the same polar term, so their difference has smooth $\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}$ and is smooth by elliptic regularity, while lemma 13.28.1 , proposition 13.29.1 show that each model differs from its Green representative, corrected by the smooth gauge function, by a bounded function. Under this correspondence the non-pluripolar volume changes by $A_1-A_0$. The rescaling map preserves analytic models when $\lambda $ is rational, by the convention of section 5.34 : on a curve, the polar measures of analytic models are finite atomic measures with rational coefficients. If $\lambda $ is irrational, an atom $a\delta _ x$ with rational $0<a<A_0$ acquires an irrational Lelong number, so its image is not analytic in this convention. Ideal models are preserved for every $\lambda $, by ( 13.29.4 ). Proposition 13.29.5 . D21EE85B9F1F Choose a smooth real $h$ with $	heta _1=\lambda 	heta _0+\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}h$. For $p\in \mathcal M(	heta _0)$ and $q=F_{10}(p)$, C555907478C9 13.29.6  egin{equation}   egin{gathered} q=P_{	heta _1}[\lambda p-h],\ \lambda p+\min _ Yh\leq q+h\leq \lambda p+\max _ Yh. \end{gathered} \end{equation} If $U_{	heta _0}(p)=0$, then \[  q=\lambda p-h-\sup _ Y(\lambda p-h).  \] Proof. The form $	heta _1-\lambda 	heta _0$ has integral zero, so the scalar Poisson equation gives $h$, as in lemma 5.29.22 . Write $p=p_{	heta _0}(\mu )$. Scaling the competitors in the ordinary envelope shows that $\lambda p$ is an ordinary model for $\lambda 	heta _0$. The gauge construction in the proof of proposition 13.29.1 therefore makes $P_{	heta _1}[\lambda p-h]$ an ordinary model in background $	heta _1$. The curvature current of $\lambda p-h$ in this background