Use the disc distance $q(\zeta ,\eta )=\operatorname {atanh} |\phi _\eta (\zeta )|$ and the functions $c_ D,\delta _ D,k_ D,\sigma _ D$ defined in the invariant-distance section. Theorem 9.18.1 . 7F33F823B79C On a bounded convex domain $D\subset \mathbb C^ n$, the one-disc distance $\delta _ D$ is a complete metric inducing the Euclidean topology, and $\delta _ D=k_ D$. Every pair of distinct points has an extremal disc: there are $f:\Delta 	o D$ holomorphic and $0<t<1$ such that $f(0)=a$, $f(t)=b$ and $\delta _ D(a,b)=q(0,t)$. Proof. We first prove the triangle inequality. Discs used in the infimum can be chosen holomorphic on a neighborhood of $\overline\Delta $ with image in $D$, at arbitrarily small additional distance cost. Indeed, replace a given disc $f$ by $f(r\zeta )$ with $r\uparrow 1$, and replace its two interpolation nodes by their quotients by $r$. Both nodes remain in $\Delta $ for $r$ sufficiently close to one; their distance converges to its old value. Disc automorphisms then give discs $f,g$ and real nodes $0<A<B<1$ such that \[  f(0)=a,\quad f(A)=g(A)=b,\quad g(B)=d,  \] with $q(0,A)$ and $q(A,B)$ as close as desired to $\delta _ D(a,b)$ and $\delta _ D(b,d)$. To obtain these nodes, send the first pair to $0,A$ and the first node of the second pair to $A$; a rotation in its stabilizer places its second node on the real geodesic beyond $A$. This is the explicit disc-automorphism formula for $B=(A+s)/(1+As)$ when the second distance is $q(0,s)$. Put \[  L(\zeta )= rac{(\zeta -B)(\zeta -B^{-1})}{(\zeta -A)(\zeta -A^{-1})},\qquad h=g+L(f-g).  \] The apparent pole at $A$ is removable because $f(A)=g(A)$. Moreover $L(0)=1$, $L(B)=0$, and on $|\zeta |=1$ \[  L(\zeta )= rac{B+B^{-1}-2\operatorname {Re}\zeta }{A+A^{-1}-2\operatorname {Re}\zeta }\in (0,1).  \] Thus $h(\partial \Delta )\subset D$ by convexity. Its boundary image is compact; its convex hull is a compact subset of $D$. The harmonic mean formula in each real coordinate puts $h(\Delta )$ in that