ds to one as $z	o \xi $, so the distance tends to infinity, contrary to the Cauchy property. The limit lies in $D$, and the local affine-disc bound proves metric convergence. $\square$ Proposition 9.18.2 . 0A57323E9BD0 Let $D$ be bounded and convex, with $C^1$ boundary. Suppose a holomorphic map $f:\Delta 	o D$ extends continuously to $\overline\Delta $, maps the circle into $\partial D$, and has a holomorphic continuous dual $\widetilde f:\overline\Delta 	o \mathbb C^ n$ whose boundary values satisfy \[  \widetilde f(\zeta )=\zeta ho (\zeta ) \overline{
u (f(\zeta ))},\qquad |\zeta |=1,  \] where $ho >0$ is continuous and $
u $ is the outward unit normal. Then $f$ has a holomorphic left inverse $P:D	o \Delta $. In particular, for $\lambda ,\eta \in \Delta $, \[  c_ D(f(\lambda ),f(\eta ))=\delta _ D(f(\lambda ),f(\eta )) =k_ D(f(\lambda ),f(\eta ))=\sigma _ D(f(\lambda ),f(\eta )) =q(\lambda ,\eta ),  \] and $g_ D(f(\lambda ),f(\eta ))=\log |\phi _\eta (\lambda )|$. The assumptions here include existence of the stationary disc. Proof. Use the complex bilinear pairing $\langle z,wangle =\sum z_ jw_ j$ and set $G(z,\zeta )=\langle z-f(\zeta ),\widetilde f(\zeta )angle $. For fixed $z\in D$ and $|\zeta |=1$, the supporting hyperplane inequality gives \[  \operatorname {Re} rac{G(z,\zeta )}\zeta =ho (\zeta )\operatorname {Re} \langle z-f(\zeta ),\overline{
