Write $dm=d	heta /(2\pi )$ on $\mathbb T$, and use the complex bilinear pairing $z\cdot w=\sum z_ jw_ j$. All distances have the normalization $q(0,t)=\operatorname {atanh}t$. Proposition 9.20.1 . 659A580EE96C Let $A$ be the disc algebra. If a finite complex measure $\mu $ on $\mathbb T$ annihilates $A$, then $d\mu =H^*dm$, where $H$ is holomorphic, $H(0)=0$, and $\sup _{r<1}\int |H(r\zeta )|dm<\infty $. For every holomorphic function with this last bound, the radial functions converge in $L^1$ to their almost everywhere boundary values. A nonzero such function has nonzero boundary values almost everywhere. Proof. We give the analytic boundary argument, including the absolute continuity assertion. The positive Fejer kernels are approximate identities, so trigonometric polynomials are dense in $L^2(\mathbb T)$; orthogonality and this density give Parseval on circles. For $H^2$, write the Taylor series. Bounded square means are equivalent to square summability of the coefficients. Their $L^2$ Fourier sum $g^*$ has only nonnegative frequencies; $g(r\zeta )$ is its Poisson integral. The Poisson kernels are approximate identities, so convergence holds in $L^2$ and at every Lebesgue point. The latter assertion follows directly by splitting the integral into dyadic arcs about that point, using $P_ r(	heta )\leq C(1-r)/((1-r)^2+	heta ^2)$. Now let $F\in H^1$, meaning that its integral means of absolute value are bounded. Remove a possible zero at zero. Jensen's formula follows by factoring the finitely many zeros inside a circle, applying the harmonic mean identity to the zero-free factor, and using $\int \log |r\zeta -a|dm=\log \max (r,|a|)$ for each zero. It gives the Blaschke condition for its remaining zeros $a_ j$: $\sum _ j-\log |a_ j|<\infty $. Indeed the means of $\log |F|$ are bounded above by the logarithm of its bounded absolute means. The factors $b_ a(z)=(|a|/a)(a-z)/(1- ar a z)$ satisfy $|1-b_ a(z)|\leq C_ K(1-|a|)$ on each compact subdisc. Thus their product $B$ converges there, has precisely the prescribed zeros, and has modulus at most one. Moreover $|B^*|=1$ almost everywhere: $B\in H^2$, and Jensen's formula for the factors gives \[  \int -\log |B(r\zeta )|dm =\sum _ j-\log \max (r,|a_ j|)\longrightarrow 0.  \] Dominated convergence for the sum and Fatou's lemma prove the claim. An origin factor contributes only $-k\log r$ and causes no change. For each finite subproduct $B_ N$, $F/B_ N$ is holomorphic and has $H^1$ norm at most that of $F$: let the circle radius tend to one, where $|B_ N|$ tends uniformly to one, and use monotonicity of absolute means. Compact convergence and Fatou give the same bound for $G=F/B$. This function is zero free. Choose a holomorphic square root $g$ of $G$ on the disc. Both $g$ and $h=Bg$ lie in $H^2$, and $F=gh$. Their $L^2$ boundary convergence and Cauchy–Schwarz give $F(r\zeta )	o g^*h^*$ in $L^1$ and almost everywhere. In particular the $H^1$ norm equals the boundary $L^1$ norm, and the Cauchy and Poisson formulas hold with these boundary values. For a nonzero $F$, its boundary values cannot vanish on a set of positive measure. After removing an origin zero, Jensen gives $\int \log |F(r\zeta )|dm\geq \log |F(0)|$, whereas the positive parts of these logarithms have uniformly bounded integrals. Fatou applied to their negative parts would contradict this inequality if the almost everywhere boundary function vanished on such a set. Finally, annihilation of $1,\zeta ,\zeta ^2,\ldots $ says that the Poisson integral of $\mu $ has only strictly positive Fourier frequencies. It is therefore a holomorphic $H$ with $H(0)=0$. Convolution with $P_ r$ bounds its absolute means by $\| \mu \| $. The $H^1$ conclusion just proved gives strong $L^1$ convergence to $H^*$. Poisson convolution also converges weakly to $\mu $ as a measure, so $\mu =H^*dm$. $\square$ Proposition 9.20.2 . 415609592B60 Let $D$ be bounded and convex, and let $f:\Delta 	o D$ be an extremal disc for two distinct points. Normalize its nodes to $0,t$, where $0<t<1$. There is a nonzero $H\in H^1(\Delta ,\mathbb C^ n)$ such that \[  h(\zeta )= rac{H(\zeta )}{\zeta -t},\qquad \operatorname {Re}(z-f^*(\zeta ))\cdot h^*(\zeta )\leq 0 \quad (z\in \overline D)  \] almost everywhere on $\mathbb T$. The boundary covector $h^*$ is nonzero almost everywhere. This disc is extremal for every pair of its nodes; in particular $\delta _ D(f(\lambda ),f(\eta ))=q(\lambda ,\eta )$. Proof. Translate the coordinates so that $0\in D$. Let $p$ be its real Minkowski functional; it is sublinear and Lipschitz, and bounded above and below by positive multiples of the Euclidean norm. On $C(\mathbb T,\mathbb C^ n)$ put $P(u)=\max p(u)$, and let $\mathcal L$ consist of the disc-algebra maps with the same values as $f$ at $0,t$. We claim $\inf _{\mathcal L}P=1$. For the upper bound, $f(r\zeta )$ has $P\leq 1$; add $\zeta (f(t)-f(rt))/t$ to restore the second value. The correction tends uniformly to zero. For the lower bound, suppose $g\in \mathcal L$ has $P(g)<1$. Its dilations $g(r\zeta )$ with the same affine correction converge uniformly to $g$, extend past the closed disc, and still have image compactly contained in $D$. A dilation of one of these maps by a factor slightly larger than one retains its image in $D$ and attains the second target at a node smaller than $t$. This contradicts extremality. This argument uses the Lipschitz gauge $p$, without identifying it with a Euclidean norm. Put $d(\zeta )=\zeta (\zeta -t)$ and $Y=dA^ n$; then $\mathcal L=f_0+Y$ for any interpolation polynomial $f_0$. Define $\ell (sf_0+y)=s$ on the real space $\mathbb R f_0+Y$. If $s>0$, the preceding infimum gives $P(sf_0+y)\geq s$; if $s\leq 0$, the inequality follows from $P\geq 0$. Real Hahn–Banach extends $\ell $ with $\ell \leq P$. The complex functional $L(u)=\ell (u)-i\ell (iu)$ is bounded, annihilates $Y$, has $\operatorname {Re}L(f_0)=1$, and satisfies $\operatorname {Re}L(u)\leq P(u)$. Represent its coordinates by finite complex measures $\mu _ j$ on the circle. To reduce this representation to the canonical positive-functional representation, for a bounded real functional $l$ set $l^+(u)=\sup _{0\leq v\leq u}l(v)$ for $u\geq 0$. This is additive on the positive cone: split any $0\leq v\leq u_1+u_2$ as $\min (v,u_1)+(v-\min (v,u_1))$, and use the reverse inequality by adding two admissible functions. Thus $l^+$ extends positively, as does $l^+-l$, and each is represented by a finite positive measure. For a positive functional on $C(\mathbb T)$, apply the canonical representation on $\mathbb R^2$ to restriction of compactly supported functions; the resulting measure is supported on the circle. Apply this to real and imaginary coordinate functionals. Since $d\mu _ j$ annihilates $A$, Proposition 9.20.1 gives $d\mu _ j=\zeta H_ j^*dm$, with $H_ j\in H^1$. Consequently $d\mu =h^*dm$, where $h=H/(\zeta -t)$. For a covector $w$ put $p^*(w)=\max _{z\in \overline D}\operatorname {Re}z\cdot w$. The dual bound implies \[  \int p^*(h^*)dm=1.  \] Here the left side is precisely $\sup _{P(u)\leq 1}\operatorname {Re}\int u\cdot h^*dm$: the upper bound is pointwise; for the lower bound choose maximizers from a countable dense subset of $\overline D$, approximate the resulting finite measurable partition by a continuous partition of unity in $L^1(|h^*|dm)$, and take its convex combination of those points. The reverse inequality $\geq 1$ also follows by applying the functional to an interpolation sequence with $P\downarrow 1$. The bounded holomorphic map $f$ has radial boundary values by the $H^2$ argument. The corrected dilations used above and dominated convergence against $h^*\in L^1$ give $\operatorname {Re}\int f^*\cdot h^*dm=1$. Since $f^*\in \overline D$ almost everywhere, equality of the integrals forces $\operatorname {Re}f^*\cdot h^*=p^*(h^*)$ almost everywhere. One component of $H$ is nonzero, so the boundary nonvanishing part of Proposition 9.20.1 applies. Thus $f^*\in \partial D$ and $h^*$ is a nonzero supporting covector almost everywhere. To change the nodes to distinct $\lambda ,\eta \in \Delta $, multiply $h$ by the meromorphic function whose circle values are \[  \phi (\zeta )=  rac{|\zeta |^2|\zeta -t|^2}{|\zeta -\lambda |^2|\zeta -\eta |^2}>0.  \] Indeed its holomorphic expression is \[  \phi h=  rac{\zeta H(\zeta )(1-t\zeta )}{(\zeta -\lambda )(\zeta -\eta ) (1- ar\lambda \zeta )(1- ar\eta \zeta )}.  \] It annihilates all disc-algebra variations vanishing at the two new nodes, by Cauchy's formula. The same holds for bounded holomorphic variations: dilate them and correct their two values by interpolation polynomials, then pass to the integral by bounded convergence. The supporting covector remains nonzero almost everywhere. If a compact-image map $g:\Delta 	o D$ interpolated $f$ at those nodes, then for some $\varepsilon >0$ it would satisfy \[  \operatorname {Re}\int g^*\cdot \phi h^*dm \leq (1-\varepsilon )\int p^*(\phi h^*)dm <\operatorname {Re}\int f^*\cdot \phi h^*dm,  \] contradicting annihilation of $f-g$. If a disc achieved the new pair at a strictly smaller disc distance, automorphisms would normalize its nodes to $0,s$, and the new nodes to $0,u$, with $s<u$. Precomposition by $(s/u)\zeta $, followed by the latter automorphism, would give exactly such a compact-image interpolant. This proves the asserted extremality for every pair, and also injectivity of $f$. $\square$ Proposition 9.20.3 . 5DE7AB27F999 On a bounded strongly real convex domain with $C^2$ boundary, an extremal disc $f$ extends to a $C^{1/2}$ map on $\overline\Delta $, with $f(\mathbb T)\subset \partial D$. Proof. Compactness and positive real boundary curvature give a fixed interior tangent-ball radius $r_0>0$, a fixed exterior tangent-ball radius $R_0$, and a collar in which the nearest boundary point is unique. For completeness, in local boundary coordinates the real Hessian restricted to the tangent space is bounded above and below by positive constants. Taylor'